WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
All right, one more time.

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:05.910
We're now looking at a framework to help us as we try to market our products,

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.040
this is the ACCORD framework.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.575
Use it when you're designing your new product.

00:00:10.575 --> 00:00:12.750
The ACCORD framework helps us to predict

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.600
which product or products will quote "cross the chasm,"

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.980
from the early adopters in the market to the early and late majority,

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.735
and then finally the late adopters.

00:00:20.735 --> 00:00:24.400
It's an acronym that stands for advantage, compatibility,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.880
complexity, observability, risk, and divisibility.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.715
We need to maximize advantage, compatibility,

00:00:31.715 --> 00:00:36.610
observability, and divisibility, and minimize complexity and risk.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.470
ACCORD is useful anytime you design a product

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:43.040
because it gives you the levers that will help determine the product's adoption.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.405
It can also help you understand why a product may have failed.

00:00:46.405 --> 00:00:50.420
Although it doesn't give you precise guidelines of how much each factor matters,

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.880
it can give you an overall sense of how your product will

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.940
do or why your product succeeded or failed in the past.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.825
When YouTube first launched,

00:00:57.825 --> 00:01:02.080
there was another site called Metacafe in which people could post video content.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.310
Essentially, Metacafe was YouTube before YouTube was YouTube.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:10.490
So why did YouTube succeed and Metacafe eventually fail?

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.330
We can't know for a fact,

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.595
but the ACCORD framework helps us understand the likely reason.

00:01:15.595 --> 00:01:18.069
In order to view video content on Metacafe,

00:01:18.069 --> 00:01:21.445
a user had to either go to the site or download a video player.

00:01:21.445 --> 00:01:25.585
In contrast, YouTube made things less complex and more compatible.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:27.310
If you want to share a video,

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.275
you can simply share the embed code in an email the recipient would click and voila,

00:01:32.275 --> 00:01:33.850
out would come the video.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.635
So YouTube made it easier and less complicated to share the content than Metacafe,

00:01:38.635 --> 00:01:40.360
the rest is history.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.380
Google purchased YouTube for over $1.6 billion and nowadays,

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.140
YouTube is a major revenue generator for Google.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.165
Another example is satellite radio.

00:01:49.165 --> 00:01:50.950
When Sirius first launched,

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.120
satellite radio was a standalone device that had to be installed on a car dashboard.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.825
The installation included drilling a hole in the car's roof to accommodate an antenna.

00:01:59.825 --> 00:02:05.185
Initial sales were quite slow and the ACCORD framework helps us understand why.

00:02:05.185 --> 00:02:08.035
The product suffered from two major problems.

00:02:08.035 --> 00:02:10.375
The first one is incompatibility.

00:02:10.375 --> 00:02:12.490
If I have to drill a hole in my car,

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.875
the device is hardly compatible.

00:02:14.875 --> 00:02:17.280
The second problem relates to risk.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.935
Buyers had to rely on an unproven service that depended on really expensive satellites.

00:02:22.935 --> 00:02:27.290
I could spend a good amount of money installing a device that maybe I couldn't use.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.990
So when did satellite radio really take off?

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:34.760
When Sirius radios began to be integrated to the existing car radio,

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.470
the company increased compatibility and reduced the perception of risk.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:42.110
After all, if my car comes with a device already implanted,

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.100
it seems like a less risky proposition.

